
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Noted: browser [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, webapp side.

  Next: scope. Right now there is nothing in the app a user could set — the page is a login form with three elements. So
  "preferences storage" can mean anywhere from a storage module with no visible effect to a working settings panel.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether this change ships something the user can see and interact with, or just the persistence layer other
  code will call later.

  - [1mStorage module only[0m — one focused file exporting get/set/reset over a defined schema, plus tests. Nothing visible in
    the browser. Honest and small, but nothing exercises it end-to-end, so the first real caller may discover the
    interface is wrong.
  - [1mStorage module + one real preference wired in[0m — same module, plus a single genuine preference (e.g. "remember my
    username" prefilling the login field) that proves the round-trip across a page reload. Slightly more work; the
    interface gets validated by an actual consumer.
  - [1mStorage module + a settings UI[0m — adds a settings panel with multiple controls. Meaningfully more scope: new markup,
    new styling decisions, new UI state to keep in sync with storage.

  Cheap to change later: adding more preferences once the module exists. Expensive-ish: the module's interface shape,
  which is exactly what a real caller shakes out.

  [1mMy recommendation: the module plus one real preference wired in.[0m It's barely more than the module alone and it's the
  difference between a design you believe works and one you've seen work across a reload.
